#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:2
msgid "Composing tasks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:7
msgid "Composing tasks into sequences"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:9
msgid ""
"You can compose tasks into a new task that runs them in order by declaring "
"a :doc:`sequence<../tasks/task_types/sequence>` task."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:11
#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:48
msgid "For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:13
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks]\n"
"\n"
"_publish = \"poetry publish\"\n"
"release = [\n"
"  { cmd = \"pytest --cov=src\" },\n"
"  { script = \"devtasks:build\" },\n"
"  { ref = \"_publish\" },\n"
"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:24
msgid ""
"And here's an example that uses the *sequence* task type explicitly in a "
"task definition:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:26
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks._publish]\n"
"cmd = \"poetry publish\"\n"
"\n"
"[tool.poe.tasks.release]\n"
"sequence = [\n"
"  { cmd = \"pytest --cov=src\" },\n"
"  { script = \"devtasks:build\" },\n"
"  { ref = \"_publish\" }\n"
"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:40
msgid ""
"See :doc:`sequence tasks<../tasks/task_types/sequence>` specifics for more "
"information on the :code:`sequence` task type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:44
msgid "Composing tasks to run in parallel"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:46
msgid ""
"You can compose tasks to run concurrently using the :doc:`parallel<../tasks/"
"task_types/parallel>` task type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:50
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.lint]\n"
"parallel = [\"mypy\", \"pylint\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:55
msgid ""
"Sequence and parallel tasks can also be combined, by default a list inside a "
"sequence is interpreted as parallel task. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:57
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.check-code]\n"
"sequence = [\n"
"  [\"mypy\", \"pylint\"], # First these two tasks run in parallel\n"
"  \"pytest\"            # then this task runs after both complete\n"
"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:65
msgid ""
"Similarly a list inside a parallel task is interpreted as sequence task. For "
"example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:67
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.check-code]\n"
"parallel = [\n"
"  \"mypy\",\n"
"  \"pylint\",\n"
"  [ # These tasks will run in sequence, concurrently with the others\n"
"    \"build-deps\",\n"
"    \"pytest\"\n"
"  ]\n"
"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:82
msgid "Composing tasks into graphs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:84
msgid ""
"You can define tasks that depend on other tasks, and optionally capture and "
"reuse the output of those tasks, thus defining an execution graph of tasks. "
"This is done by using the ``deps`` task option, or if you want to capture "
"the output of the upstream task to pass it to the present task then specify "
"the ``uses`` option, as demonstrated below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:86
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks]\n"
"_website_bucket_name.shell = \"\"\"\n"
"  aws cloudformation describe-stacks \\\n"
"    --stack-name $AWS_SAM_STACK_NAME \\\n"
"    --query \"Stacks[0].Outputs[?(@.OutputKey == 'FrontendS3Bucket')]."
"OutputValue\" \\\n"
"  | jq -cr 'select(0)[0]'\n"
"\"\"\"\n"
"\n"
"[tool.poe.tasks.build-backend]\n"
"help = \"Build the backend\"\n"
"sequence = [\n"
"  {cmd = \"poetry export -f requirements.txt --output src/requirements."
"txt\"},\n"
"  {cmd = \"sam build\"},\n"
"]\n"
"\n"
"[tool.poe.tasks.build-frontend]\n"
"help = \"Build the frontend\"\n"
"cmd = \"npm --prefix client run build\"\n"
"\n"
"[tool.poe.tasks.shipit]\n"
"help = \"Build and deploy the app\"\n"
"sequence = [\n"
"  \"sam deploy --config-env $SAM_ENV_NAME\",\n"
"  \"aws s3 sync --delete ./client/build s3://${_bucket_name}\"\n"
"]\n"
"default_item_type = \"cmd\"\n"
"deps = [\"build-frontend\", \"build-backend\"]\n"
"uses = { _bucket_name = \"_website_bucket_name\" }"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:118
msgid ""
"In this example the :code:`shipit` task depends on the :code:`build-"
"frontend` :code:`build-backend`, which means that these tasks get executed "
"before the :code:`shipit` task. It also declares that it uses the output of "
"the hidden :code:`_website_bucket_name` task, which means that this also "
"gets executed, but its output is captured and then made available to the :"
"code:`shipit` task as the private variable :code:`_bucket_name`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:120
msgid ""
"Normally the key in the uses table will be set as an environment variable "
"for the task, but if as in this example it has the form of a private "
"variable (lowercase and prefixes with ``_``) then it will only be available "
"to reference in config (or in a cmd task), and will not be available on the "
"environment (which prevents it from being used directly in a ``shell`` task)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:124
msgid ""
"Note that captured output that is exposed as an environment variable via the "
"``uses`` is compacted to have new lines removed. This is similar to how "
"interpolated command output is treated by bash."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:127
msgid "Sourcing variables from dependency task output"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:129
msgid ""
"Whereas ``uses`` captures task output into a named variable, the "
"``uses_env`` option captures the task output and parses variables from it "
"like an *env file* (dotenv syntax) and exposes the resulting variables to "
"the task at runtime. This lets a single upstream task provide zero or more "
"variables, which it names itself, rather than the host task naming one "
"variable per task."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:131
msgid ""
"This is useful for loading several related variables produced together by "
"one command, such as credentials from a tool like ``aws-vault``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:133
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks._aws-creds]\n"
"cmd  = \"aws-vault export ${_profile}\"\n"
"args = [{ name = \"_profile\", default = \"dev\", help = \"AWS profile to "
"use\" }]\n"
"\n"
"[tool.poe.tasks.deploy]\n"
"help = \"Deploy with credentials sourced from aws-vault\"\n"
"cmd = \"terraform apply\"\n"
"uses_env = \"_aws-creds --profile ${_profile}\"\n"
"args = [{ name = \"_profile\", default = \"dev\", help = \"AWS profile to "
"use\" }]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:145
msgid ""
"Here ``_aws-creds`` returns several ``AWS_*=...`` lines, which are then "
"exposed in the environment of the ``deploy`` task. ``uses_env`` also accepts "
"a list of task invocations (including parameter expansions), applied in "
"order so that later entries override earlier ones. Notice that the ``_aws-"
"creds`` task optionally accepts an argument to pass to aws-vault to choose "
"which AWS profile to use."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/composition_guide.rst:149
msgid ""
"As is conventional in poethepoet, lowercase variables prefixed with ``_`` (e."
"g. ``_my_private_var``) are considered private — accessible within task "
"config but not exposed on the subprocess environment at runtime."
msgstr ""
